# Round hostsimd2: Muse Spark's post-expert norm folded into the host union's own sums. The layer holds the norm
# (HostLayer::set_post_norm, the rule model::ops::PostNorm), and the union's sum pass and the one-column leg's sum
# take each expert's down through it before its weight (PostNorm::add_normed: the same products, scale and adds
# as the old norm-in-place then weighted add, so every column is the same bits; gpu lib
# a_post_norm_layer_norms_each_experts_row_before_its_weight pins it, now with a wide call whose sums run over the
# pool). Gone from the prompt call: HostRun::experts_post's single-thread copy of x into one column a routed slot,
# the zero-fill of the slot rows, the per-slot norm and the weighted sum, and the union's 8x quantize of the copied
# columns and its copy of the store into the slot rows. Also in the tree, both 0 on this term [derived]: the
# IQ2_XXS 8-column tile restructured as the wide tile's one group (no engine path reaches it: compute_rows sends
# every IQ2_XXS run of two or more columns to the wide tile), and its tile_units row (every unit of the union's
# passes is an IQ2_XXS row, so only the row's fixed/per-column ratio enters the cut, and the steal blocks absorb it).
# Against int039 6faa40c4c (iq2simd's wide tile and the xstream prompt stream in).
# Decomposition per layer, pp512, host slots n_h (4096 when nothing streams; xstream's split keeps fewer) [derived]:
# - experts_post, one thread, at n_h = 4096: x copied into 4096 columns, 67 MB stored with write-allocate at the
#   core's 15..20 GB/s -> 3.4..4.5 ms; the slot rows zeroed, 67 MB -> 2.7..4.5 ms; the norm and sum read the rows
#   (67 MB from the pool's writes at ~25 GB/s) and write them back -> 4..6 ms; out zeroed, 8.4 MB -> 0.4 ms.
#   10.5..15.4 ms.
# - The pool's share: 7/8 of x's quantize (58.7 MB read) 0.5..0.7 ms, the store-to-rows copy's 67 MB of writes
#   0.6..0.9 ms; the norm now in the pool's sum pass +0.05 ms. 1.0..1.5 ms.
# - Saved 11.5..16.9 ms a layer at n_h = 4096, x n_h / 4096 under a split.
# Base: iq2simd's 127..192 ms a layer (7.9..11.9 s a pass) with nothing streamed -> +6.3..+15.3 %; a split that
# keeps half the slots (n_h ~ 2048) and a 5..8 s pass -> +4.7..+11.6 %. Band +4..+15 %. pp4096 the same band
# (eight ubatches of the same calls), printed beside it.
kind: ab
question: does folding the post-expert norm into the union's sums take experts_post's single-thread copy,
  zero-fill, norm and sum (and the union's 8x x quantize and its store copy) off Muse Spark's prompt pass?
term: pp tok/s @ P = 512, prose prompt (corpus-prose.ids), Muse Spark UD-IQ2_XXS, A6000 + host, place a, serve
  defaults (--ctx-size 4160) - (hostsimd2 - base) / base, arms rotated by round in one invocation
unit: %
predict: +4..+15
rounds: 2
condition: one lease, one invocation from /Users/hckim/repo/bloomery-hostsimd2 (it builds this tree's
  bloomery-serve) after the base tree's own release build of bloomery-serve at int039 6faa40c4c (a clean tree, e.g.
  bloomery-int039base): BLOOMERY_MODEL=muse-spark BLOOMERY_AB_ROUNDS=2 BLOOMERY_BOX_ENV='DEPTH_SERVE_N=64
  BLOOMERY_LEASE_CARD=docs/cards/hostsimd2-pp.card' just depth-serve bloomery-int039base:512 bloomery-hostsimd2:512
  bloomery-int039base:4096 bloomery-hostsimd2:4096. Both arms hold the same plan (host code only; the musespark
  union-columns patch, if applied, moves only host slab bytes), so their plan, pass and xstream lines print the
  same card experts, pass rows and streamed experts, and their tg rows agree within the window's ~5 %. The xstream
  records give n_h per layer (4096 less the streamed slots), which places the result in the band. A row marked
  [cold], [cpu-busy] or [other-busy] is void, and so is a window whose majflt differs by more than the load's own
  scatter between arms. Box time 13..32 min [derived: iq2simd's sitting, the same arms and prompts, plus the two
  serve builds]
decide-in: land it; the host term per layer is then rows (93..145 ms at n_h = 4096) beside O' ~3..5 ms (x quantize,
  combines, sums, joins) and the residual iq2simd's O left unexplained: price that residual first, from one
  BLOOMERY_PROFILE=1 run's host_x_quant, host_gate_up, host_h_quant, host_down and host_sum against the layer's wall,
  then open the rows lever (the IQ2_XXS signs on the weight side once a run, ik's form, ~-25..-30 % of the rows)
decide-below: under +4 %: experts_post was off the wall (the layer card-bound under the split, or the core's stores
  ran faster than 15..20 GB/s); read n_h from the xstream records and union_ms per layer from one
  BLOOMERY_STEP_STATS=1 run of each arm; land it at >= 0 (same bits, a smaller host call), and the miss names the
  term: the split's balance or the copy rate
decide-above: past +15 %: the copies cost more than derived (write-allocate under 15 GB/s, or the 67 MB slot rows
  evicting the union's working set from L3); re-derive O from the profile parts and price O' and the card's ~11 ms
  serial share next
